\f1\fs22 The Parliament House of the Elvi had started life as the Clambake brothel, which explained why the committee room had a rotating bed and mirrors that reflected poor life choices. The High Elvis sat at the head of the conference table\'97formerly the "Champagne Jacuzzi Deluxe"\'97wearing a jumpsuit that cost more than most small wars and somehow made it look reasonable.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Brothers and sisters," he began, his voice carrying the weight of a man who'd seen too much gospel and not enough gyration, "we face the greatest threat since the Great Sideburn Shortage of '89."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta slumped in his chair, which kept trying to massage him in places he didn't know could be massaged. Around the table sat the Elvi high command: Colonel Kalamazoo, Major Memphis, and Specialist 5 Tulsa McLean, whose rifle was bedazzled for the occasion.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"The gospel plague spreads," the High Elvis continued. "Yesterday, I saw Elvi 4,792 reading a Bible. Not eating it, not using it as a coaster\'97reading it. With comprehension."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Blasphemy against The Dawkins!" Major Memphis slammed his fist on the table, causing several hidden compartments to pop open and offer various lubricants.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Peace, brother." The High Elvis raised a ring-encrusted hand. "The One teaches us that anger is just evolution's way of saying you're not thinking hard enough."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta rubbed his temples. "Look, Your... Highness? I appreciate the promotion to Great Teacher and all, but this is way above my pay grade. I clean things. I write the occasional lyric about not hitting people. I don't do religious warfare."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"You do now." The High Elvis fixed him with a gaze that had launched a thousand hip swivels. "The One After the One grows in the Holy Momma's sacred womb. The Dawkins himself, blessed be his bibliography, spoke of this day."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"He did?" Chanta asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Metaphorically. Chapter seven of 'The Selfish Gene,' if you squint and have had enough whiskey." Colonel Kalamazoo pulled out a heavily annotated copy, covered in sequins and doctrinal disputes. "See here: 'Memes spread through the meme pool.' Clearly referring to our music spreading through the cultural waters of Ulyssia."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"That's not what\'97"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"And here!" Major Memphis had his own copy, this one with musical notation in the margins. "'Ideas have spreading power.' Obviously about amplifier technology."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta watched the theological discussion with the hollow feeling of a man who'd accidentally started a religion by rhyming "selection" with "affection" while drunk.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Brothers!" The High Elvis stood, his cape unfurling dramatically despite the lack of wind. "We cannot defeat the gospel corruption with mere arguments. We need something stronger. Something that speaks to the very core of being."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Violence?" suggested Specialist 5 Tulsa McLean hopefully.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Music, brother. Evolutionary music. The kind that makes natural selection sexy." The High Elvis began to pace, which in the confined space meant everyone had to dodge his cape. "I propose we take our message beyond the Ghetto. Beyond even the Pale. We shall hold the greatest concert in history... at the Domain."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The room fell silent except for the ambient hum of the massage chairs.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"The Domain?" Major Memphis whispered. "But that's... that's Airborne territory. They have grass. Real grass. That judges you for stepping on it."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Exactly." The High Elvis smiled. "We'll show all of Ulyssia that evolution isn't just for Elvi. It's for everyone. Even people who summer in Monaco and winter in their own self-importance."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"How?" Colonel Kalamazoo adjusted his sunglasses, which he wore despite being indoors and underground. "The booking fees alone would bankrupt a small nation. The Airborne charge by the blade of grass."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta found himself speaking before his brain could object. "I'll pay for it."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Everyone turned to stare.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"It's just beer money to me now," he continued, the words feeling strange in his mouth. Having 18.1935% of the world's wealth did that to vocabulary. "I could buy the Domain. Hell, I could buy the concept of domains."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Praise be to the Great Teacher!" Major Memphis fell to his knees. "Truly, The Dawkins works through your bank account!"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"But money isn't enough," the High Elvis said. "We need political approval. The forms alone require forms to fill out forms. It's bureaucracy all the way down."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Sarah could help," Chanta said quietly. "The Holy Momma has Eloise Shakti's ear. And Eloise has everyone else by their everythings.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Then it's settled!" The High Elvis clapped his hands, producing a sound like thunder wearing jewellery. "The Rock Against Revival shall happen! Great Teacher, you'll write new songs for the occasion. Songs that'll make Darwin himself rise from Westminster Abbey and wear his Jailhouse Socks.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony's stomach performed a move that would have impressed even the Elvi. "About that... I can't."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Can't?" The word hung in the air like a disco ball in a morgue.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"I haven't written anything since..." Chanta paused, not wanting to admit he'd been creatively constipated since leaving Sarah's presence. "The muse isn't speaking to me."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Then we must bring you closer to the source!" Colonel Kalamazoo declared. "The One After the One clearly inspires through proximity! It's basic evolution\'97adapt to your environment or die lyrically barren!"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"I'd need to be near Sarah. Near Little Clinton." Chanta's voice cracked slightly on her name. "But she's at Sublime Point, and I'm persona non grata there."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Then we must\'97" The High Elvis was interrupted by Chanta's tablet playing a ringtone that sounded like money having an identity crisis.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta looked at the screen. Unknown number, but somehow still radiating smugness.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"I should take this," he said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"The Great Teacher has spoken!" Major Memphis declared. "Pause the revolution for telecommunications!"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta stepped out into the hallway, which was lined with what appeared to be erotic wallpaper featuring Elvis in various states of undress with historical figures. Charles Darwin featured prominently, usually holding a guitar.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He answered the call, already knowing this would make everything more complicated.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Behind him, he could hear the High Elvis beginning a sermon about the evolutionary advantages of rhythm and blues. Something about sexual selection and hip mobility.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta had a feeling his pay grade was about to get even more complicated.\

